1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
Someone asks, what gradient to set up the
Mises would you recommend?

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:08,400
I would say usually, Adam or gradient
descent with momentum are pretty good,

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:11,279
and a lot of the time you should consider
using gradient clipping.

4
00:00:11,279 --> 00:00:16,559
The next part of our overall roadmap is to
discuss what happens when we have rounding
error,

5
00:00:16,559 --> 00:00:18,960
when we have underflow, overflow,

6
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:24,240
all of these issues that happen when we
use a finite number of bits to represent
real numbers.

7
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:26,320
This is really important.

8
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:30,480
I know it sounds boring to talk about this
low-level stuff with bits,

9
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:34,480
but writing numerically precise code is
actually a deep learning super skill.

10
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:39,600
A lot of the time you'll find that deep
learning algorithms sort of work when you
code them up.

11
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:42,480
The loss will go down, the accuracy will
go up,

12
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:45,760
the accuracy will get within a few
percentage points of the state of the art,

13
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:50,880
and you can check your code really
carefully, and you can find that there
aren't really any bugs,

14
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,560
but maybe there are some things that
aren't quite optimal about it.

15
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:58,800
That's often caused by a loss of numerical
precision,

16
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:00,800
where you run your gradient descent
algorithm,

17
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:03,280
and you don't get quite of good as results
as you think you should.

18
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:08,719
Another time you'll find that your deep
learning algorithm explodes,

19
00:01:08,719 --> 00:01:10,320
you get a lot of not-in-number values,

20
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:12,320
you get a lot of very large values,

21
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:15,200
and this might happen even if you make
your learning rate relatively small,

22
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:16,640
and you think everything should be stable.

23
00:01:17,599 --> 00:01:22,640
Both of these problems actually usually
come from writing code that doesn't have
high enough

24
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:23,680
numerical precision.

25
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:29,359
So being able to write numerically precise
code can really differentiate how well you
perform

26
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,280
as a deep learning practitioner.

27
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:35,200
A lot of people have heard the story of
how when I first thought of generative
adversarial nets,

28
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,400
I was at a bar, and I went home, and I
coded them up immediately.

29
00:01:38,879 --> 00:01:42,240
And within an hour or so later, I actually
had them working.

30
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:45,359
A lot of people have wondered how I was
able to do that,

31
00:01:45,359 --> 00:01:51,439
and part of the answer is that I knew
about all of this really boring numerical
precision stuff.

32
00:01:51,519 --> 00:01:54,640
So I didn't waste any time shooting myself
in the foot,

33
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:56,719
writing numerically and precise code,

34
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:00,560
and that reduces the amount of time it
takes to get the algorithm working a lot.

35
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:08,319
The basic idea is that when you use a
finite number of bits to represent a real
number,

36
00:02:08,319 --> 00:02:11,680
you get both rounding errors and what are
called truncation errors.

37
00:02:12,319 --> 00:02:15,919
In a digital computer, we're usually
talking about using float32,

38
00:02:16,479 --> 00:02:19,840
or some other similar schemes to represent
real numbers.

39
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:23,039
That's especially true when we're using
CUDA on GPUs.

40
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:28,080
Sometimes we use other floating formats. A
lot of the problems I'm going to describe
here

41
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:32,560
will often mostly go away if you're able
to switch to float64,

42
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:37,599
but that usually requires slower
computation on GPU,

43
00:02:37,599 --> 00:02:41,439
or may even force you to move to CPU. It
will also force you to use more memory.

44
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,840
So if you can write numerically precise
code using float32,

45
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:47,439
then that can be a big advantage.

46
00:02:47,439 --> 00:02:56,639
There's basically three categories of
problems that happen on these kinds of
32-bit representations.

47
00:02:56,639 --> 00:02:59,120
So first, most numbers that you work with,

48
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:01,840
they're not going to cause catastrophic
problems on their own,

49
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:03,520
but they are going to get rounded.

50
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:07,840
If you have some real number x and you
turn it into a representation on computer,

51
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:10,879
it's going to get rounded to some value x
plus delta,

52
00:03:10,879 --> 00:03:15,520
where delta is just some small value
describing the rounding error that
happened,

53
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,159
that you'll be off by a little bit from
what you meant to be.

54
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:24,400
A larger problem is when you have some
extremely large value of x,

55
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:29,760
you just can't represent it with the range
of numbers that your float32 values can
represent.

56
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:34,000
And it will actually get replaced by some
placeholder, like,

57
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,200
inf, just saying that x is too large to
represent.

58
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:40,640
And so now it's not really a finite value
anymore.

59
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:42,640
You've got this infinite number,

60
00:03:42,719 --> 00:03:48,000
and your code will probably still go ahead
and keep doing arithmetic on this number
as if it

61
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
were a real value, and then you'll get in
trouble.

62
00:03:51,919 --> 00:03:55,359
There's another topic which is still
basically a kind of rounding error,

63
00:03:55,359 --> 00:03:58,079
but it's a particularly harmful kind of
rounding error.

64
00:03:58,079 --> 00:04:02,000
If you have a very small value of x, it
can get replaced by zero.

65
00:04:02,879 --> 00:04:07,119
This is especially harmful because a lot
of the algorithms in deep learning often
assume that

66
00:04:07,119 --> 00:04:11,199
we have some very small but non-zero
variable.

67
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:15,119
Like, for example, if you use the logistic
sigmoid activation function,

68
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:19,039
you can pass very negative values to the
logistic sigmoid.

69
00:04:19,759 --> 00:04:23,199
And algebraically, they'll never become
quite zero,

70
00:04:23,199 --> 00:04:25,839
but in a computer they can definitely get
rounded down to zero.

71
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:31,360
And so people are asking, aren't these
numerical issues already addressed in the
current

72
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:37,120
deep learning libraries? And the answer is
basically, yes, but you need to know when
to look out

73
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:42,879
for them. A lot of the time you write your
own code, and you might end up rewriting a
version

74
00:04:42,879 --> 00:04:48,480
of something that already has an
numerically stable variant. Sometimes you
might need to,

75
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:53,280
for example, compute a softmax over
several tensors, instead of just one
tensor,

76
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:57,280
and you'll need to know how to do that in
an numerically stable way if your library
only

77
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:05,920
provides a softmax for a single tensor.
Some other people are asking if there's
any pointers on

78
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,879
the best way to deal with nands. That's
basically what the next several slides
will do.

79
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:12,960
Someone asked, how does the 64-bit
representation solve the nand error?

80
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:17,439
Well, the basic idea is that if you have
32 bits, there's a certain range of values
that you can

81
00:05:17,439 --> 00:05:27,920
represent. If you have 64 bits, that range
expands. So a program that might cause a
nand when you

82
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:34,319
use 32 bits might actually avoid the nand
when you use 64 bits. If you use a program
that uses

83
00:05:34,319 --> 00:05:38,399
even bigger numbers, you can eventually
still get nands for 64 bits. It's just
that

84
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:46,319
fewer programs cause problems using 64
bits than using 32. One example is if you
look at the

85
00:05:46,319 --> 00:05:52,480
Pylon 2 repository that I used to work on
in grad school before we had tensor flow,
you can look

86
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:57,680
at some of the unit tests, and when I
compute the k-old divergence between two
Gaussian distributions,

87
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:04,720
if I use float 64, the unit test expects
the k-old divergence to have a minimum
value of 0.

88
00:06:06,079 --> 00:06:13,360
But if I use float 32, the unit test
actually has to allow the k-old divergence
to go slightly negative.

89
00:06:14,399 --> 00:06:19,920
So one example of how we can get rounding
errors and they can affect this code is
what happens when

90
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:25,439
we add a very small number to a larger
number. So we're going to make an array
using 32-bit floats,

91
00:06:25,759 --> 00:06:31,360
and we're going to store the value 0 and
the value 10 to the minus 8 in this array.
If we compute

92
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:39,279
the argmax of this array, the argmax is 1
because position 0 has a value of 0,
position 1 has a

93
00:06:39,279 --> 00:06:44,720
positive but very small value of 10 to the
minus 8. So now suppose that we take these
numbers and

94
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:51,279
we add them to a much larger number, we
add them to 1. Well, 0 is going to have 1
added to it and

95
00:06:51,279 --> 00:06:58,959
end up with a value of 1. Ideally, 1 plus
10 to the minus 8 would be represented as
1, but

96
00:06:59,679 --> 00:07:05,679
it's at 1 point, you know, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0,
1 after the correct number of 0s. In this
case, it's

97
00:07:05,679 --> 00:07:10,719
actually going to get rounded to 1, which
isn't quite what we want. When you take
the argmax of

98
00:07:10,719 --> 00:07:16,559
the newer array where we added 1 to it, we
actually get that the argmax is 0 now
because NumPy breaks ties

99
00:07:17,519 --> 00:07:24,000
to the first index that's tied for the
max. So this addition of a number that
caused rounding

100
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:29,280
error has actually downstream changed the
results quite a lot. We only really wanted
to extract a

101
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:33,920
single bit from this computation and we're
now actually getting the value of that bit
wrong.

102
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:41,199
There's some secondary effects that happen
when we have rounding error and overflow
and

103
00:07:41,199 --> 00:07:46,079
underflow errors. What happens is after
the first error, we do some more math and
then the second

104
00:07:46,079 --> 00:07:52,159
error causes a bigger problem. So for
example, so we have some code that
computes x minus y.

105
00:07:52,879 --> 00:07:58,319
x minus y is a relatively safe thing to do
to start with. There's definitely some
ways that

106
00:07:58,319 --> 00:08:02,800
can go wrong, but there's not anything
fundamentally wrong with doing a
subtraction. But now suppose

107
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:09,759
that x overflows to nth, y overflows to
nth, well then we've got infinity minus
infinity and we

108
00:08:09,759 --> 00:08:15,039
don't really know which infinity is
larger. This kind of operation is not
really defined. So now

109
00:08:15,039 --> 00:08:21,599
our x minus y will turn these nth into
nth. A lot of the time when you have
overflow, the way that

110
00:08:21,599 --> 00:08:27,439
you first see it is that you'll get a nth
in your loss function or your gradient. So
somewhere in

111
00:08:27,439 --> 00:08:32,879
your computational graph, you'll get an
nth and then by the time it actually gets
printed out or

112
00:08:32,879 --> 00:08:37,120
plotted somewhere, it will have been
turned into a nth by these additional
steps of arithmetic that

113
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:43,679
came afterward. One of the main functions
that's often the culprit of numerical
precision problems

114
00:08:43,679 --> 00:08:52,879
in deep learning programs is the x
function that computes the natural
logarithms base e raised to

115
00:08:52,879 --> 00:08:58,320
the power of x. This will overflow for
large values of x. And a lot of the time
when I say large

116
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:04,080
values, I am talking about extremely large
values. For x, when I say large values,
I'm actually talking

117
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:11,280
about numbers that are not very big. If
you're using float32, a value of 89 past
2x will overflow to n.

118
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:18,000
To make sure that your program doesn't
have trouble with x, just never use large
x as the argument

119
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:24,480
for x. And make sure that you design the
structure of your functions that you
compute so that they will

120
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:30,800
never actually pass a large x to x. A lot
of the rest of this lecture will be about
ideas for how you

121
00:09:30,799 --> 00:09:38,240
can prevent that from happening in
specific cases. The other big problem with
x is that if your

122
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:44,559
argument to it is very negative, x of x
can underflow. This may not be a problem.
If you think about it,

123
00:09:45,599 --> 00:09:51,599
x of a very negative number is just some
small number epsilon. And if you round
that number down to

124
00:09:51,599 --> 00:09:57,039
zero, you've only made a mistake of size
epsilon. So if that was your final output
of the function,

125
00:09:57,039 --> 00:10:02,559
it's not a very big error. Unfortunately,
if you then do other math on top of this
output,

126
00:10:03,279 --> 00:10:08,799
rounding a small number epsilon down to
zero can be catastrophic if you're using x
of x in a

127
00:10:08,799 --> 00:10:13,519
denominator or as an argument to a
logarithm. And so on, it can cause really
big problems.

128
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:21,679
Another case where you can sometimes
encounter danger is when you have
subtraction, especially if

129
00:10:21,679 --> 00:10:28,639
you want to guarantee that the output of
the subtraction is going to be positive.
So if you have two

130
00:10:28,639 --> 00:10:33,919
values x and y, and you know algebraically
that you can prove that x is always
greater than y,

131
00:10:34,639 --> 00:10:39,679
in a computer, you may actually find that
x minus y ends up being negative. So if
you rely it on

132
00:10:39,679 --> 00:10:45,599
x minus y being positive in subsequent
steps of your program, you can run into
trouble. One case

133
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:50,560
where you see this a lot is computing the
variance. So there's one way that you can
write the

134
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:56,399
variance where it's the expectation of a
squared value. If you implement the
variance this way in

135
00:10:56,399 --> 00:11:02,560
your code, it's always going to give you
positive numbers because the output of the
the squaring value

136
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:10,160
will will always be non negative. I
shouldn't say positive, sorry. And then if
you look at the

137
00:11:10,159 --> 00:11:17,360
difference between the expectation of the
squared value of the function and the
square of the

138
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:22,240
expected value of the function,
algebraically that's a perfectly valid way
of rearranging this

139
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:26,959
expression for the variance. And a lot of
time this is actually the one where we we
actually have

140
00:11:26,959 --> 00:11:32,959
access to those values to compute it.
Unfortunately, it's numerically unstable
because if we have a

141
00:11:32,959 --> 00:11:37,199
little bit of rounding error on the left
and a little bit of rounding error on the
right, and these

142
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:41,280
values were very close to zero to start
with, if the rounding error goes in the
wrong direction,

143
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:46,960
we can end up with a small negative value
instead of zero. And that can cause
trouble, especially

144
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:50,800
if you were planning to pass this value to
a square root or a logarithm later on.

145
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:59,040
Logger them in square root are also
functions where you want to be very
careful. The logarithm of

146
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:04,960
zero is negative infinity and the
logarithm of a negative number is
imaginary, usually in software

